":"https://democracy.eastdevon.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-10-14","committee":"Council","time":"6.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.eastdevon.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-10-29","committee":"Scrutiny Committee","time":"6.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.eastdevon.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-11-04","committee":"Cabinet","time":"6.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.eastdevon.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-11-12","committee":"Housing Overview and Scrutiny Committee (formerly Housing Review Board)","time":"10.00 am","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.eastdevon.gov.uk"}],"recent":[{"date":"2026-07-30","committee":"South and East Devon Habitat Regulations Executive Committee","time":"2.00 pm","status":"Cancelled","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.eastdevon.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-03","committee":"Scrutiny Committee","time":"6.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.eastdevon.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-16","committee":"Cabinet","time":"6.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.eastdevon.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-16","committee":"Cabinet","time":"6.00 pm","status":"Cancelled; Extraordinary","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.eastdevon.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-17","committee":"Council","time":"6.00 pm","status":"Confirmed; Extraordinary","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.eastdevon.gov.uk"}]},"news":[],"news_drafts":[{"headline":"East Devon faces permanent \u00a33.8m budget deficit as government cuts funding","lead":"East Devon Council faces permanent budget deficits of \u00a33.861m in 2027/28 and \u00a34.722m in 2028/29, driven by government decisions to cut funding allocations. These are not temporary pressures but permanent reductions in the money available to the council. The council plans to use reserves \u2014 money set aside \u2014 of between \u00a32.7m and \u00a33.9m to close the 2027/28 gap. However, the council's own financial strategy identifies that \"the key risk is that short-term balancing decisions in 2027/28 create a structural problem for 2028/29 and beyond.\" The planned savings meant to prevent this remain limited. Planning fee reforms are expected to deliver \u00a31.4m but are prudently assumed at \u00a30.5m pending legislation due at year-end; a leisure contract renegotiation may deliver at least \u00a30.5m.","iso":"2026-09-16","paper_title":"Annual complaint monitoring and service improvement report 2025-26","paper_url":"https://democracy.eastdevon.gov.uk/documents/s31677/31677.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"East Devon faces \u00a33.8m budget deficit driven by permanent loss of government funding","lead":"East Devon faces a structural budget deficit of \u00a33.861m in 2027/28 and \u00a34.722m in 2028/29, driven by permanent reductions in government funding including business rate rebasing and the ending of New Homes Bonus. The Cabinet has acknowledged the council's financial position has \"fundamentally changed through external factors beyond the Council's control.\" To balance its budget, the council plans to draw down \u00a32.7m to \u00a33.9m from reserves in 2027/28, alongside an additional \u00a33m in reserve requests. However, the financial strategy explicitly warns that \"the key risk is that short-term balancing decisions in 2027/28 create a structural problem for 2028/29 and beyond.\" Identified savings plans \u2014 including planning fee reform and leisure service negotiations \u2014 are uncertain and dependent on external legislative and contractual decisions, falling significantly short of the deficits being forecast.","iso":"2026-09-16","paper_title":"Annual complaint monitoring and service improvement report 2025-26","paper_url":"https://democracy.eastdevon.gov.uk/documents/s31677/31677.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"East Devon faces \u00a34m annual budget shortfall from government funding cuts","lead":"East Devon District Council faces structural budget deficits of \u00a33.9m in 2027/28 and \u00a34.7m in 2028/29, driven by permanent reductions in government funding including business rate rebasing and the ending of New Homes Bonus. The council plans to cover the gap by drawing down reserves\u2014reducing its general fund balance from 18 months of spending cover to 12 months\u2014but savings schemes remain uncertain: planning fee reform could raise \u00a31.4m but the council prudently estimates only \u00a30.5m pending legislation, leisure service savings depend on contract negotiations still unresolved, and a capital review offers only modest returns. The strategy explicitly warns that \"short-term balancing decisions in 2027/28 create a structural problem for 2028/29 and beyond.\" Meanwhile, housing complaints rose sharply from 372 to 462 at stage one of the complaints process, with 80% upheld or partly upheld; the Housing Ombudsman found two counts of severe maladministration in leasehold damp and mould cases, with 28 cases still open and compensation liability unknown.","iso":"2026-09-16","paper_title":"Annual complaint monitoring and service improvement report 2025-26","paper_url":"https://democracy.eastdevon.gov.uk/documents/s31677/31677.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true}],"findings":[{"iso":"2026-09-24","committee":"Audit and Governance Committee","paper_title":"Internal Audit Plan Progress Report - Quarter 1 2026-27","paper_url":"https://democracy.eastdevon.gov.uk/documents/s31787/31787.pdf","headline":"Council Maintains Zero Overdue Audit Actions Despite 70 Open Items","detail":"As of 25 August 2026, the council has no overdue Priority 1 or Priority 2 actions. 11 actions have been closed since 1 July 2026, demonstrating that management is maintaining remediation momentum.","category":"governance","matters":"Keeping actions on schedule demonstrates management responsiveness and effective execution on audit remediation, mitigating the impact of outstanding control weaknesses.","figures":"0 overdue actions against 70 open total; 11 actions closed since 1 July 2026","quote":"As of 25 August 2026, the Council has no overdue actions. 11 actions have been closed since 1 July 2026.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-24","committee":"Audit and Governance Committee","paper_title":"Internal Audit Plan Progress Report - Quarter 1 2026-27","paper_url":"https://democracy.eastdevon.gov.uk/documents/s31787/31787.pdf","headline":"Information Governance and Performance Management Limited Audits Remain Unresolved After 2+ Years","detail":"Both 2023/24 Limited assurance audits continue unresolved: Information Governance (4 open), Performance Management (4 open). This suggests ongoing management execution or resource constraints in addressing identified control weaknesses.","category":"governance","matters":"Multi-year persistence of unresolved audit findings indicates systemic management effectiveness issues and may constrain the council's ability to deliver corporate priorities and meet compliance obligations.","figures":"Information Governance: 4 open of 13 total; Performance Management: 4 open of 7 total","quote":"Information Governance Policies and Compliance with Retention Policy Limited 2023/24 4 0 5 4 13 and Performance Management Limited 2023/24 4 0 2 1 7","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-24","committee":"Audit and Governance Committee","paper_title":"Internal Audit Plan Progress Report - Quarter 1 2026-27","paper_url":"https://democracy.eastdevon.gov.uk/documents/s31787/31787.pdf","headline":"Contract Management Limited Assurance Audit Has 7 Open Actions; Area Now Designated Strategic Risk","detail":"The 2025/26 Limited assurance audit identified significant procurement control gaps. 7 of 10 agreed actions remain open as of 25 August 2026. Contract management has been added as a new strategic risk (Gov 26) on the council's risk register.","category":"audit","matters":"Contract management is core to procurement control; persistent open actions expose the council to supplier management and procurement risk.","figures":"7 open actions out of 10 total from 2025/26 audit","quote":"Contract Management Limited 2025/26 7 0 1 2 10","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-24","committee":"Audit and Governance Committee","paper_title":"Internal Audit Plan Progress Report - Quarter 1 2026-27","paper_url":"https://democracy.eastdevon.gov.uk/documents/s31787/31787.pdf","headline":"Disrepair Claims Audit Completed with Limited Assurance and 8 Priority 2 Actions","detail":"A new audit of disrepair claims management was completed in Q1 2026/27 with Limited assurance opinion, generating 9 agreed actions including 8 Priority 2 ('important findings that need to be resolved by management').","category":"audit","matters":"Disrepair claims represent potential landlord negligence liability; Priority 2 actions indicate important findings requiring management resolution and potentially costly remediation.","figures":"9 total actions: 8 Priority 2, 1 Priority 3","quote":"Disrepair Claims Complete Limited Medium 9 0 8 1","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-24","committee":"Audit and Governance Committee","paper_title":"Internal Audit Plan Progress Report - Quarter 1 2026-27","paper_url":"https://democracy.eastdevon.gov.uk/documents/s31787/31787.pdf","headline":"Housing Legionella Compliance \u2013 12 Open Actions Remain Unresolved","detail":"The 2025/26 Limited assurance audit identified significant control gaps in legionella management. As of 25 August 2026, 12 actions remain open. A follow-up audit has been prioritized for 2026/27 with actions due to be completed in-year.","category":"audit","matters":"Legionella management is a statutory safety obligation; 12 open actions from a Limited assurance opinion represent material compliance exposure with potential liability implications.","figures":"12 open actions as of 25 August 2026","quote":"Housing Landlord Compliance \u2013 Legionella Limited 2025/26 12 0 2 0 14","material":true}],"editorial":null,"freshness":{"last_paper_iso":"2026-09-16"},"name":"East Devon","daily_post":{"iso":"2026-09-22","since_iso":"2026-08-23","distress":false,"headline":"Material unquantified costs for new town preparation and leisure contract implications are held outside the MTFP","lead":"Significant costs being held outside the main forecast could materialize during the MTFP period and worsen the deficit if not absorbed by additional savings or borrowing","also":["15 other findings in this window"],"watch":"Housing Overview and Scrutiny Committee (formerly Housing Review Board) on 2026-09-23","n_findings":16,"n_documents":0,"top_paper_title":"Financial Strategy Appendix A","top_paper_url":"https://democracy.eastdevon.gov.uk/documents/s31750/31750.pdf","draft":true},"findings_total":60,"news_drafts_total":3}}